## The standing criticism of the last attempt

The conjecture [9862340011f9effe] misreads the rule. The rule says "The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text." The phrase "when any fence holds one" means a fence holding exactly one object; Reply A's fence holds two objects, so the condition is false and the else branch applies, making the top-level last object {"c": 3} the correct score. The code's recover_json_object does exactly that: the fenced candidate is the entire content between fences, which is not a single JSON object, so it is passed over, and the top-level scan returns {"c": 3}. The conjecture's expectation that Reply A should score {"b": 2} assumes the rule means the last object among several inside a fence, which the words do not say. A next conjecture should test a fence holding exactly one object, or no fence, and avoid repeating the assumption that multiple objects inside a fence count as 'one'. The reading [54da7fb33cdcfa0c] names the function and expectation the prose stated, but the execution [2f1ce9dcff50b600] is unrunnable because the pair kernel is not a verdict function; that is a separate defect from the conjecture's misreading.

